    "detail": "The deploy of the last commit failed to type check. account-test.ts and workspaces-test.ts both declare `failures` and `check` at the top level and neither imports anything, so TypeScript treats them as global scripts rather than modules and the two declarations collide.\n\nIt does not surface when either runs, because tsx compiles one file at a time. It surfaces in `next build`, which checks the whole project, so the first place anybody sees it is a failed deploy. workspaces-test.ts had been fine on its own for weeks; adding a second file with the same shape is what broke it.\n\nBoth carry `export {}` now, and scripts-are-modules.ts checks the rest. It is in the offline suite, so the next script written without an import fails locally rather than on Vercel.\n\nThe reason I did not catch it is worth writing down. I verified with\n\nnpm run build 2>&1 | grep -E \"Failed|✓ Compiled\" | head -1\n\nand \"✓ Compiled successfully\" is printed before \"Failed to type check\", so head -1 returned the success and discarded the failure. A check that reads only the first matching line of a build log can report only good news. Exit codes, not greps."
