    "date": "2026-08-30",
    "title": "Harden the deployment without touching the way in",
    "detail": "Five things, none of which change how signing in works.\n\nRoutes now check for a session themselves instead of inheriting it from the proxy. The proxy is one regular expression away from being wrong, and a matcher edit that accidentally excluded a path would have silently opened it. /api/chat spends money and /api/anthropic-workspaces relays a key to Anthropic, so a mistake there costs more than a redirect.\n\nThe sign-in page was an open redirect. `from` arrives in the query string, so /signin?from=https://example.com produced a genuine Eterneon sign-in page that landed you somewhere else afterwards, which is the shape every credential phishing page wants. Only same-site paths are accepted now, protocol-relative and backslash forms included.\n\nRequest bodies are read through a byte counter with a 20MB ceiling rather than buffered blind. Content-Length is checked first as a cheap rejection and then ignored, because a chunked request can lie about it; the guard test proves a body claiming to be ten bytes still gets refused.\n\nA CSP, Permissions-Policy, COOP, and CORP are set, with frame-ancestors none and connect-src self. script-src keeps unsafe-inline, which is the one weak directive and a deliberate one: the App Router emits per-page inline bootstraps that hashes cannot cover, and the nonce alternative needs the proxy, which does not run on the sign-in page. Verified against a live page with no violations rather than assumed.\n\nWorkspace mutations are capped at 200 ops and validated against the set of tables applyMutations can actually write, since an unknown table fell through its switch silently and made a typo look like a save.",
